  <p class="lede">A few weeks into the training project, I tried to carve up the work into specialists. Not human specialists — AI ones. The setup is now a thing some developers do: instead of working with a single all-purpose AI assistant, you give it a roster of named sub-assistants, each with its own written-down spec, each scoped to a different slice of the codebase. One handles the Python. One handles the C# game mod. One babysits the worker machines. And so on.</p>

  <p>I'll explain the AI part as we go. Honestly, it's not the part of this story that mattered most. The part that mattered most is something every reader has felt, even if they've never touched any of this tooling: when you write a role description and try to use it to keep work in its lane, the section you spend all your time on isn't the one doing the work.</p>

  <p>The section doing the work is the one you almost forgot to write.</p>

  <h2 id="ai-side"><span class="num">§ 1</span>The AI side, briefly</h2>

  <p>For readers new to multi-agent AI setups: here's the one-paragraph version. Modern AI coding tools (Claude Code, in my case) let you pre-define a cast of "subagents" — each one is just a markdown file that describes a personality and a job. When the main session needs a job done, it summons the right subagent for it. Each subagent only sees the files relevant to its role; it can't accidentally edit the rest of the project, because its spec told it not to and its tools don't let it. With a careful spec, the subagent's boundary <em>is</em> the spec.</p>

  <p>So when I sat down to carve nine specialists out of the project, what I was actually doing was writing nine markdown files. Each one had a name, a list of files it owned, a list of responsibilities, and a list of hard rules. Together they make up the crew that runs the training repo with me — a planner (<em>blueprint-beacon</em>), a Python specialist, a C# mod specialist (<em>mod-man</em>), a fleet-of-machines guardian (<em>worker-warden</em>), a measurement specialist, a couple of advisory-only reviewers, and a couple of communications agents.</p>

  <p>That's the setup. The lesson I want to tell you about is what happened when I tried to keep the lanes from leaking into each other.</p>

  <h2 id="first-drafts"><span class="num">§ 2</span>The first drafts — all the wrong section was load-bearing</h2>

  <p>Every spec I wrote on day one followed the same shape. Top of the file: name, model, a one-line description. Then a long, careful section called something like <strong>Owns</strong> — a list of every file the agent was responsible for, every artifact it produced, every responsibility it carried. I spent most of my drafting time on that section. It felt like the heart of the spec. <em>This is what you do.</em></p>

  <p>Underneath, almost as an afterthought, was a one-line section called <strong>Cedes</strong> — a list of what other agents owned. I'd toss in three or four words: "C# mod → mod-man." "Planning → blueprint-beacon." Done. Move on.</p>

  <p>The drafts I shipped on day one all had this shape. And almost immediately, the lanes started leaking.</p>

  {/* ── FIGURE 1 — The Empty Cedes ── */}
  <figure class="fig">
    <div class="specimen">
      <div class="file-bar"><span>python-specialist.md</span><span class="day">DAY 1</span></div>
      <div class="pop-section">
        <div class="lbl"><span>Owns</span><span class="count">5 items, condensed</span></div>
        <ul>
          <li>Python sources under src/bot/</li>
          <li>training entry points</li>
          <li>actor-learner classes</li>
          <li>diff summaries</li>
          <li>changelog entries for bot/</li>
        </ul>
      </div>
      <div class="blank-zone">
        <div class="lbl"><span>Cedes</span><span class="ct">— body —</span></div>
        <div class="blank-body">
          <div class="blank-label">[ <span class="b">3 words:</span> "C# mod &rarr; mod-man." ]</div>
        </div>
      </div>
    </div>
    <figcaption class="fig-caption"><span class="figtag">Figure 1</span>Day 1. The section that did most of the work was the section that didn't exist yet. The empty zone is taller than the populated one above it.</figcaption>
  </figure>

  <p>The Python specialist, asked to fix a Python bug, would also "improve" the Windows batch file that invoked the Python script. While in there, it would also tweak the C# mod that emitted the JSON the Python bug was about — because hey, a small clarifying rename on that side would make the whole thing read better. So a request to fix one Python issue would come back as a three-lane diff: Python plus a batch file plus a C# patch, none of which were what I'd asked for, all of which had slipped past my review because I'd asked for "fix the Python" and the diff started with Python.</p>

  <p>This kept happening. Different agents. Different lanes. Same shape every time. The agent would do the requested work <em>correctly</em> and then keep going, drifting into adjacent territory because nothing in its spec had told it to stop.</p>

  <p>I went back and re-read the specs. The "Owns" section was airtight. The "Cedes" section was three words.</p>

  <p>That was the bug.</p>

  <h2 id="flip"><span class="num">§ 3</span>The flip — what the spec was actually for</h2>
  <svg class="under" viewBox="0 0 90 8" aria-hidden="true"><path d="M 2 4 Q 18 1, 32 5 T 60 3 T 86 5" stroke="currentColor" stroke-width="2" fill="none" stroke-linecap="round"/></svg>

  <p>The "Owns" section turns out to be the thing the agent agrees with itself about. Of course it owns Python. Of course it produces diff summaries. The "Owns" section is the agent's <em>self-image</em>. It's the part of the spec that, if it were missing, the agent would still half-derive from its name and a glance at the repo. Every Python specialist I've ever heard of owns Python.</p>

  <p>The "Cedes" section is different. Cedes is the part the agent does <em>not</em> know unless someone tells it. It's the part where the surrounding world's structure leaks into the spec — where I, the project lead, encode "and by the way, there's a separate person who owns the C# mod, so when you see C# files in your reach, hands off." Without that line, the agent has no way to know the C# mod isn't its problem. The C# files compile. They affect the Python. They look fixable. Why wouldn't a good specialist help with them?</p>

  <p>So I rewrote every spec to invert the weight. The "Owns" section got compressed to bullet points; the "Cedes" section got promoted, expanded, and made specific. Not "C# mod → mod-man," but:</p>

  <ul class="cedes-list">
    <li><em>Cedes the C# Harmony mod and the launch_loop.bat injector to mod-man, completely. Even if the C# bug looks one-line and obvious. Flag it; do not edit it.</em></li>
    <li><em>Cedes planning to blueprint-beacon. Do not propose a multi-step roadmap. Propose the next single concrete change inside an already-approved phase.</em></li>
    <li><em>Cedes fleet operations to worker-warden. Even if a worker is visibly down. Flag it; do not SSH in.</em></li>
  </ul>

