  <p>Each spec got its negative space spelled out — what the role <em>doesn't</em> do, named in plain language, with the hard cases ("even if it looks obvious") explicit. After that the lane-leaking mostly stopped. Not entirely — the seams where two lanes touch are their own story — but the easy class of leak, the one where an agent helpfully wandered into adjacent territory, went away.</p>

  <p>The spec wasn't a description of the role. It was a fence around the role. And the fence is built out of the negative space.</p>

  <h2 id="same-shape"><span class="num">§ 4</span>Where I started to see the same shape everywhere</h2>

  <p>Once you notice the shape once, it's hard to stop noticing it. I started looking back at things I'd worked with before this project and seeing the same pattern in places that had nothing to do with AI agents.</p>

  <p><strong>Job descriptions.</strong> The best job descriptions I've ever seen — the ones where the person hired actually does the job that was advertised — have a section that names what the role <em>isn't</em>. "This is not a managerial role; you will not be running a team." "This is not a research role; you will not be publishing." "This role does not own the deploy pipeline; that's [Other Team]." Those JDs are rare. Most JDs are pure positive space — a list of responsibilities, a list of qualifications, a list of nice-to-haves — and the people hired into them spend their first six months gradually discovering which adjacent territory they're expected not to touch by accidentally touching it and being told. The negative space is in the company; it's just not in the JD. The new hire has to reconstruct it from gossip.</p>

  <p><strong>Project charters.</strong> Every project I've worked on that finished anywhere close to its planned scope had an explicit out-of-scope section. "We are not migrating the auth system. We are not redesigning the billing flow. We are not addressing the legacy import path." Every project that ballooned into a six-month odyssey had a charter that said, more or less, "we will improve the user experience." Improve which user experience? Improve it by how much? Improve it for whom? The positive scope is just a flag in the ground. The out-of-scope list is the actual fence.</p>

  <p><strong>Code-review rubrics.</strong> The good ones name what <em>not</em> to bring up in the review. "Don't litigate the variable names; the team has agreed on a convention. Don't flag missing tests on this PR; the test plan is in a separate ticket. Don't propose architectural changes; this PR is a bug fix and an architectural change is a separate conversation." Without those nots, every PR review collapses into a debate about everything, because every reviewer's mental list of things-worth-bringing-up is unbounded by default. The reviewers know what <em>can</em> be a code-review concern. They need the team to tell them what's <em>not</em> one this week.</p>

  <p><strong>Pull-request templates.</strong> Same shape. The template that says "What does this PR do? What does it not do?" gets you a description of the change. The template that just says "What does this PR do?" gets you a sales pitch.</p>

