  </figure>

  <p>It's the same fence in every case. The work the role does is the agent's self-image — easy to write down, partially derivable from the title, the section that feels load-bearing. The work the role <em>doesn't</em> do is the contract with the surrounding system. It's the part the surrounding system needs you to know, that nothing about your role tells you on its own.</p>

  <h2 id="harder"><span class="num">§ 5</span>Why the negative space is harder to write</h2>

  <p>Writing positive responsibilities is easy because the answer is in the role. <em>What does a Python specialist do? Python.</em> <em>What does a senior backend engineer do? Senior backend engineering.</em> You can stare at the title and produce three bullet points without leaving your chair.</p>

  <p>Writing negative responsibilities is harder because the answer is in the <em>organization</em>, not the role. To write "this role does not own the deploy pipeline," I need to know there's a deploy pipeline, that it's owned, by whom, and that the boundary between this role and that owner is somewhere I expect new hires to bump into. That's four facts about the surrounding system, none of which are in the role itself. The reason most JDs and most project charters and most agent specs leave the negative space empty isn't laziness. It's that nobody gathered the four facts.</p>

  <p>Once I'd noticed this, the path forward stopped being a writing problem and started being a <em>cataloging</em> problem. The reason my specs had no real negative space on day one wasn't that I didn't believe in negative space. It's that I hadn't sat down and made a list of every adjacent lane and named who owned it. The negative-space section couldn't be specific until I'd done that work.</p>

  {/* ── FIGURE 4 — The Lane Table with the Empty Third Column ── */}
