    <figcaption class="fig-caption"><span class="figtag">Figure 4</span>The lane table on Day 1. The first two columns are easy: every spec already has them. The third column is the work — and on Day 1, it's almost all blank.</figcaption>
  </figure>

  <p>For the AI crew, "doing that work" looked like writing a one-page table of who owned what across the whole repo, then walking each agent's spec line by line and asking <em>given this table, what does this agent decline?</em> The first table took maybe an hour. After that the negative-space sections wrote themselves, because the answer for each agent was just a query against the table.</p>

  <p>I think this generalizes. The reason the good JDs and the good project charters are rare isn't that the people writing them are smarter. It's that someone, somewhere upstream of those documents, sat down and made the lane table. Once the table exists, every fence on every role is just a query.</p>

  <h2 id="takeaway"><span class="num">§ 6</span>The takeaway, in one line and then in seven words</h2>
  <svg class="under" viewBox="0 0 90 8" aria-hidden="true"><path d="M 2 4 Q 18 1, 32 5 T 60 3 T 86 5" stroke="currentColor" stroke-width="2" fill="none" stroke-linecap="round"/></svg>

  <p>The line: when you're scoping any role — a contractor, a hire, a project, a PR, an AI agent, a team — the load-bearing part of the spec is the section that names what the role doesn't do, and the section is only useful if it's specific.</p>

  <div class="principle">
    <p class="quote">Positive space is the headline.</p>
    <p>The headline gets you attention. The contract gets you the work back inside its lane.</p>
    <p>It took me about a day and a half to learn this from a roster of nine markdown files that kept leaking. I think I would have learned it faster from the first job description I ever wrote, except that one didn't have a negative-space section at all, and so I never noticed which section I'd skipped.</p>
  </div>

